cification. Since I don't have access to the actual PRD content, I'll need to reference it with a glob pattern in the required_reading section and let the system resolve the correct file, then anchor my requirements to whatever structure the PRD actually uses. Looking at the testutils PRD structure, I'd expect requirement categories for the DiffTest types, test execution mechanics, and comparison/reporting features. But I'm second-guessing myself on inventing IDs—the constraint about not inventing design decisions means I should let the stitch agent map the architecture-described capabilities to actual PRD requirements rather than guessing at IDs that might not exist. I'm going to use `prd004-testutils` as my reference since that follows the established naming pattern, and the stitch agent will resolve the actual PRD file anyway by searching through the glob pattern. Let me check if there's any other clue about the actual PRD numbering in the use case structure, but it looks like the only documented PRDs are prd002-sys, prd003-format, and prd011-magefiles. the actual file anyway. Looking at the architecture, testutils should have its own PRD since it's a shared library component with requirements spanning multiple utilities, and the progress already notes the spec is complete. So I'll go ahead and propose the core task: implementing the DiffTest struct and differential test runner as a combined types-plus-implementation task that fits within the 150-250 line budget.\n\nI'm defining the DiffTest struct with all the necessary fields (args, stdin, env, expected exit code and output), then building a runner that executes both binaries with identical inputs and compares their stdout, stderr, and exit codes to produce structured results. I'll also add support for normalization hooks to handle known acceptable differences, and I'm using os/exec to invoke the binaries as specified in the architecture.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the scope—the constraint about splitting impleme