rison logic, and normalization all in one file, which fits the 150-250 target. I have exactly 4 requirements as specified, and each task is self-contained with all the context it needs from required_reading.\n\nLooking back at the PRD numbering, testutils came earliest in rel01.0, while sys and format were in rel02.0 with PRD numbers 002 and 003 respectively. There's no prd001 reference anywhere in the context, so prd004-testutils seems reasonable, though I should note that the stitch agent might need to verify the actual file path since the numbering could be non-sequential. I'll use a glob pattern in the required_reading to be flexible about the exact PRD filename. I'm settling on the file paths and noting that the stitch agent can locate the correct files if the exact names don't match. Now I'm finalizing the YAML structure and making sure there are no formatting issues like unescaped colons in the strings.\n\nI'm laying out the task requirements, starting with the necessary documentation to review—the testutils PRD, use cases, test suites, architecture notes, and code style references—then specifying the files to create and the key requirements for the DiffTest struct, including fields for test inputs, stdin, environment variables, binary paths, and GNU diff configuration. I'm settling on difftest.go as the primary file since DiffTest is the main exported type, following the project's naming convention. I'll match the style and structure of existing utility packages like terminal.go and size.go, keeping in mind this is a test helper library that won't produce a standalone binary. The acceptance criteria confirm I need to export DiffTest, DiffResult, and RunDiffTest, with RunDiffTest handling the actual binary execution and comparison logic. Everything checks out against the requirements and conventions, so I'm ready to finalize the response.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22067,"c